  "style": {
    "id": "ship-in-bottle",
    "name": "Ship in a Bottle",
    "description": "A bright modeller's bench in daylight: a large clear glass bottle lies on a wooden cradle, and a small wooden sailing ship (walnut hull with a red boot and a cream strake, three masts, pleated paper fan sails, thread rigging) is built outside the bottle with its masts hinged flat against the deck. The long thin bundle slides through the narrow neck on a wire, settles onto a blue putty sea, and one thread running out of the neck is pulled: the masts swing upright fore to aft, the sails fan open and the rigging draws taut. A cork goes in. One plan (rigging.js) turns thread length pulled into every hinge angle in closed form, places every thread point (taut or sagging by its slack), carries the insertion path, proves in Node that the folded bundle clears the neck and the raised ship clears the glass, and writes the score.",
